    description: "README.md already points to SECURITY.md (line ~29, \"Audited IO surface\"). Add a short plain-language security/trust section so a colleague deciding whether to install hypergumbo on a personal machine gets the material facts up front:\n- the code is largely AI-agent-written (~349k production + ~508k test LOC as of 2026-10) and has not been human-reviewed line by line;\n- what the default `hypergumbo .` reads/writes (the repo read-only; ~/.cache/hypergumbo; no network) and which commands touch the network or execute code (install-*, add-extras, install-embeddings, build-grammars compiles C, the rust-analyzer backend runs the analysed repo's build.rs, tracker sync, htrac serve);\n- SECURITY.md's claims are checked by hypergumbo itself (self-claims gate), with named blind spots -- \"confirmed_with_caveats\" is not proof; the independent allowlist gate (if landed) as a second check;\n- the tracker package (htrac) is not yet covered by the audit (until its self-claims item lands);\n- analysing an untrusted repository: what it can influence (in-repo .hypergumbo.toml catalogue data is off unless opted in per ADR-0061; backend trust per ADR-0045);\n- how to run confined (container, or network blocked for its Python + fs_usage/strace once).\nKeep claims exact and dated; update the section as the self-claims and allowlist-gate items land. Owner request 2026-10-07."  # 8bd6
    fields: {}  # 8bd6
